<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Vulci</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 280 BCE (approx.) · capital Vulci</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Wealthy Etruscan city near the Fiora river whose tombs held thousands of Greek vases and whose François Tomb preserves an Etruscan version of early Roman history.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Vulci, Etruscan Velch, grew from Villanovan villages on a plateau above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiora-river">Fiora</a> river, some 10 km from the coast. By the 7th and 6th centuries BCE it had become one of the richest cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>, importing luxury goods and controlling a large territory reaching towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-bolsena">Lake Bolsena</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Vulci was a major centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-working">bronze-working</a>, exporting tripods, vessels and candelabra across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. Its tombs yielded an extraordinary number of Athenian painted vases, many excavated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucien-bonaparte">Lucien Bonaparte</a> in the 19th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-tomb">François Tomb's</a> paintings show the Vulcian heroes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caelius-vibenna">Caile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulus-vibenna">Avle Vipinas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mastarna">Macstrna</a>, whom the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome's</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/servius-tullius">Servius Tullius</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> defeated Vulci together with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/volsinii">Volsinii</a> in 280 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius-coruncanius">Tiberius Coruncanius</a> celebrated a triumph over both. Much of its coastal land was confiscated and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> colony of Cosa founded there in 273 BCE. The city declined into a minor Roman town and was abandoned in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/vulci.jpg" alt="Vulci" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Robin Iversen Rönnlund · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vulci_site.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 280 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vulci</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Etruscan religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Etruscan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Velch</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-590&amp;focus=vulci" aria-label="Vulci on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4962 669 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Vulci lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5282 925L5290 918M5282 925L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5290 918h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5282 925h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5282 925h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-590&amp;focus=vulci">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

